[42.223619, "o", "\u001b[35m_\u001b[0m\u001b[35minternal.cli.main import main\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[35mModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[35m```\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[35mThe `pip` launcher scripts (`/usr/local/bin/pip`, `pip3`, `pip3.13`) were fine — only the module they import was missing.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[35mNote this was *not* a PATH, permissions, or network problem.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[35m## The fix\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[35m1. Moved the stale metadata aside (`pip-24.3.1.dist-info` → backup) so the installer wouldn't think pip was already installed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[35m2. Reinstalled from the bundled wheel that ships with your Python:\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[35m```bash\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[35mpython3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[35m```\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37mThis installed pip 24.3.1 from \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32m/usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m — no network needed.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[42.223653, "o", "\u001b[34m##\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[34mVerification\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m Check \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m Result \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[42.224146, "o", "\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m---\u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m---\u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mpip --version\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mpip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m ✅ \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mpython3 -m pip --version\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m works ✅ \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mimport pip\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m loads from \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32m/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip/__init__.py\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m ✅ \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mvenv\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m + \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mpip install six\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m installed and imported successfully ✅ \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mpip install six\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m system-wide \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\u001b[37m installed, \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mimport six\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m worked ✅ \u001b[0m\u001b[37m|\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[37mI then uninstalled the tes"]
[42.224224, "o", "t package and removed the temp backup, so the system is back to a clean state apart from the repaired pip. I also scanned all site-packages for other \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32m.dist-info\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m folders with missing code — \u001b[0m\u001b[1;33m**\u001b[0m\u001b[1;33mnone found\u001b[0m\u001b[1;33m**\u001b[0m\u001b[37m, so pip was the only casualty.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[34m##\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[34mTwo things worth knowing\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[42.224569, "o", "\u001b[37m-\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[1;33m**\u001b[0m\u001b[1;33mUse a virtualenv for real work.\u001b[0m\u001b[1;33m**\u001b[0m\u001b[37m You're root on a Debian-based image, and pip itself warns that writing to the system prefix can conflict with the OS package manager. Your source-built 3.13 isn't apt-managed so it's lower-risk, but \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mpython3 -m venv .venv\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m is still the safer habit.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[37m-\u001b[0m\u001b[37m \u001b[0m\u001b[1;33m**\u001b[0m\u001b[1;32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[1;32m/usr/bin/python3.11\u001b[0m\u001b[1;32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[1;33m also has no pip\u001b[0m\u001b[1;33m**\u001b[0m\u001b[37m — but that's Debian's own interpreter and Debian deliberately ships it without pip/ensurepip. If you ever need it, use \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mapt install python3-pip\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m rather than \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mensurepip\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m, since apt installs it into the Debian-managed location. Your default \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mpython3\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m on PATH is the 3.13 one, so this doesn't affect normal use.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[42.224846, "o", "\u001b[37mTo avoid a repeat, don't \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mrm -rf\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m a package directory to uninstall it — use \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mpip uninstall <pkg>\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m, or if things get wedged, \u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[32mpip install --force-reinstall <pkg>\u001b[0m\u001b[32m`\u001b[0m\u001b[37m.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[42.305942, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@4a41ae59535a:/app# "]
[47.075892, "i", "\u0004"]
[47.076006, "o", "\u001b[?2004l\r\r\nexit\r\n"]
